---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = "/alternatives/sendlane-alternative";
const title = "Sendlane alternatives for Shopify: continuity and channel handover";
const desc = "Evaluate Sendlane and Privy continuity, subscription workflows, SMS routing and replacement readiness before changing suppliers.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Has Privy acquired Sendlane?",
    "answer": "Yes. Privy announced the acquisition in February 2026. Confirm the transition plan for your own account."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does acquisition mean my account is closed?",
    "answer": "The announcement alone does not establish account closure, a universal deadline or completed migration of every workflow."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I stay?",
    "answer": "Stay when the actual account, support and cost meet your requirements and any proposed transition is understood."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I cancel SMS before porting?",
    "answer": "Confirm dependencies with both providers first. Source service and access may be required until the receiving route is ready."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I discard quiet automation branches?",
    "answer": "Check event history and subscription, refund and cancellation fixtures before removing a branch with a rare but necessary job."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is a lower invoice enough to switch?",
    "answer": "Include labor, channel and storefront dependencies, overlap and rebuild cost, then require operational acceptance."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Migration decision guide</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p></header>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Start with the actual Sendlane account and Privy relationship</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">Privy announced its acquisition of Sendlane on February 2, 2026</a>. That establishes ownership, not a universal shutdown date or proof that every account, contract and workflow has already moved. Ask your account contact for the written plan applying to your store: login, billing entity, support, sending infrastructure, exports and any required transition. Keep a working account when its economics, automation and support meet your needs.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.sendlane.com/">Sendlane presents email, SMS, reviews and forms</a>, while <a href="https://www.privy.com/">Privy markets email, SMS, list growth and automation</a>. Evaluate the functions you actually use. Support and consolidated customer work can remain valuable after onboarding. There is no sound basis for assuming all teams outgrow Sendlane, must schedule calls for routine edits or receive a guaranteed renewal discount.</p>
<h2>Separate continuity, repair and replacement</h2>
<p>For continuity within the supplier group, request a store-specific migration specification rather than assuming Sendlane and Privy are interchangeable interfaces. For a repair, demonstrate the failed segment, campaign or integration in the existing account and agree an owner. For an external replacement, define the unresolved requirement and test it in the candidate account. A higher renewal invoice alone does not describe the work or message volume being purchased.</p>
<p>Klaviyo, Omnisend and Drip are candidates to investigate for Shopify lifecycle requirements; Rejoiner is a broader retention platform with optional services, not just a narrow cart fallback. Postscript is a candidate when SMS is the specific job. ActiveCampaign or Brevo may enter an evaluation for a defined workflow, without implied Shopify parity. <a href="https://www.sequenzy.com/">Sequenzy's SaaS email positioning</a> warrants checking relevant email needs, not assuming a complete ecommerce replacement. Require the same fixtures from every shortlisted supplier.</p>
<h2>Build a continuity register before accepting a transition</h2>
<p>Record each account ID, store integration, billing commitment, renewal or notice date, sender domain, SMS number, form destination, review widget and administrator. Add the owner and support ticket for every unresolved dependency. Ask which assets remain accessible, which must be exported and which require rebuilding. Keep the supplier's answer alongside the register; public acquisition announcements cannot answer account-level questions.</p>
<p>Separate service work from product features: who prepares campaigns, monitors deliverability, changes exclusions, produces creative and handles urgent failures? Assign each task to an internal owner or a contracted provider before ending existing support. An approved campaign scheduled after the transition needs an explicit new owner and disposition. Preserve open support cases so a new team does not repeat diagnosis from scratch.</p>
<h2>CPG renewal rehearsal: prove the renewal case or the exit case</h2>
<p>Proposed exercise, not a merchant success story: a CPG store reviews its email and SMS arrangement at renewal. Prepare controlled profiles for a new one-time buyer, an active subscriber, a paused subscription, a recent cancellation and a refunded order. Compare the current account and proposed destination using the same product, order and subscription events. No invented monthly revenue, renewal quote or recovery percentage is needed to make the decision.</p>
<p>Test welcome from each form source without restarting existing subscribers. A one-time buyer may qualify for a replenishment reminder; an active subscription customer should follow the merchant's separate policy instead of receiving a redundant repurchase offer. A paused subscriber needs a deliberate resume or exclusion rule. Cancellation should not be mistaken for permission to send any promotion. Verify order cancellation, refund and out-of-stock handling immediately before send.</p>
<p>Now delay a subscription update and replay an order webhook. The final customer state must control the message and duplicate events must not produce extra sends. Record event identifiers, timestamps, expected audience, actual message and the receiving owner's explanation. Do not delete an apparently quiet branch until its role in these edge cases is understood.</p>
<h2>Move consent, preferences and waiting work together</h2>
<p>Export available contacts, channel-specific permission evidence, opt-out history, bounces, complaints, topic preferences and suppression lists. Preserve source and timestamps where available. An email subscriber is not automatically an SMS subscriber; imported membership is not evidence of new permission. Resolve uncertain records before enabling the corresponding channel. Test unsubscribe and preference updates in both directions for any supported overlap arrangement.</p>
<p>List contacts waiting inside each automation and their completed steps. Choose whether the incumbent finishes, the destination resumes eligible remaining work, or the step is cancelled. Preserve remaining delays and already-issued offers rather than restarting from entry. For an order placed during cutover, reconcile purchase time and event arrival before releasing a recovery message. A scheduled broadcast also needs a single sending owner and an audience refreshed for recent opt-outs.</p>
<h2>SMS continuity is a separate dependency</h2>
<p>Do not cancel a source SMS account simply to start a port. Confirm number ownership, authorization, destination support, registration requirements, routing and lead time with both providers. Keep source access and service dependencies until the receiving provider confirms readiness. If the number cannot transfer, design a separate approved sender transition and customer-support plan rather than assuming a new number inherits old routing.</p>
<p>Document who receives replies to old campaigns, handles opt-outs, monitors pending conversations and resolves support requests. Test an inbound reply and an opt-out through the actual supported route before launch. Reconcile suppression state before each provider can send. Rollback after a port may require provider assistance; do not promise a toggle back to the old account. Email can move on a different schedule if dependencies permit.</p>
<h2>Forms, reviews and integrations can outlive an email switch</h2>
<p>Inventory popups, embedded forms, hidden seasonal displays, theme clones, review requests, published reviews and widgets actually used. Decide which remain, move or retire. Confirm export and import support for review content and metadata before removing a widget. A replacement email editor does not itself replace published storefront content. Test product associations and destinations where review workflows are involved.</p>
<p>For each Shopify, subscription, loyalty, helpdesk or custom integration record the writer, event name, identity key, destination and retry policy. Verify consent and source tags from a controlled form submission. Remove stale list IDs and webhook destinations only after the receiving path passes. Give every trigger one active sender; receiving events in a disabled destination is useful for testing without duplicating customer messages.</p>
<h2>Use equivalent quotes and explicit acceptance gates</h2>
<p>Request current written quotes for your actual sends, stored or billable contacts, SMS destinations and segments, support scope and services. Include review or capture dependencies, overages, overlap and rebuild labor. Avoid comparing an old bundled invoice with a software-only quote. Check notice and export terms before setting a cancellation date; read-only access should be confirmed rather than promised for a fixed archive period.</p>
<p>Hypothetical arithmetic: $600 monthly fee savings minus five extra operator hours at $60 leaves $300 monthly benefit. A $3,600 migration takes twelve months to recover at that rate, before revenue changes. If extra work rises to ten hours, the recurring benefit is zero. Test price and labor sensitivity with actual proposals; do not fund the move with assumed winback uplift.</p>
<p>Release each channel only after permissions, waiting steps, duplicate prevention, replies, forms and the CPG fixtures pass. Assign rollback ownership and stop thresholds using account history and provider guidance. Keep incumbent entries disabled until destination sends and newly delivered messages are reconciled if rollback is needed. Match attribution windows, returns and promotion periods when comparing reporting; platform-attributed orders are not proof of incremental revenue. Keep the current setup when the proposed move fails these gates.</p>
<h2>Related reading</h2>
<p><a href="/alternatives">alternatives</a> · <a href="/compare/sendlane-vs-klaviyo">sendlane vs klaviyo</a> · <a href="/compare/rejoiner-vs-sendlane">rejoiner vs sendlane</a> · <a href="/use-cases/deliverability">deliverability</a> · <a href="/compare/drip-vs-sendlane">drip vs sendlane</a> · <a href="/apps/sendlane">sendlane</a> · <a href="/compare/postscript-vs-sendlane">postscript vs sendlane</a></p>
</article></div></section>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Sendlane alternatives FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
